WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.772
Thank you so much for coming on the show today.

2
00:00:03.772 --> 00:00:05.572
Oh, it's my pleasure.

3
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:07.410
My pleasure, so nice to see you.

4
00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.960
I have this strange feeling

5
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.680
as if you've never not been on the scene.

6
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.010
You've been a part of these big, sweeping movies.

7
00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.980
I mean, "The Butler" is such a big deal.

8
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.280
It broke all those records.

9
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.350
It's such an important, true story.

10
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:22.710
You worked with Oprah Winfrey,

11
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.330
and she's an executive producer

12
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.700
on your new film, "The Water Man".

13
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.950
What is it like working with such a legend?

14
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.320
You know, my relationship with her began

15
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.430
with us playing mother and son,

16
00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.940
and that relationship sort of bled

17
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.920
beyond being in front of the camera.

18
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.240 line:15%
She just continued to be a sort of mother figure in my life,

19
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.810 line:15%
a great, you know, source of advice for me,

20
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.270 line:15%
advocacy for me, encouragement for me.

21
00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:54.440
We share a sensibility and, yeah, she's just been

22
00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.400
one of my my life's greatest blessings, you know,

23
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.690
from being together in "The Butler".

24
00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.400
She was one of the main reasons "Selma" even happened.

25
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.150
It was when I asked her to come onboard as a producer

26
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.160
that that really sort of gained traction.

27
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.080
And then, in terms of "The Water Man",

28
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.260
it was something I went to her

29
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.760
and told her I was passionate about,

30
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.060
and she said, "Look, I'm gonna do everything I can

31
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.800
to help you realize this dream."

32
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.580
I do feel like, when she ordains things in any way

33
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.090
as something we're all supposed to pay attention to, we do.

34
00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.450
We want people to shine light on stories, history,

35
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.860
you know, whether it's fiction or non-fiction stories.

36
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.050
I mean, she started with the book club.

37
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:41.050
Stories are so important, and you know,

38
00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:46.263
I heard that "E.T." was a film you saw very young.

39
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.513
What, may I ask, did that film do for you?

40
00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.030
Well, I have to admit, this is incredibly surreal

41
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.510
for me to be talking to you about this,

42
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:03.423
because that film was so formative in my life.

43
00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.860
It's super weird to be talking to you about it

44
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.780
and that Oprah is my friend.

45
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.821
I mean, how weird is life?

46
00:02:10.821 --> 00:02:13.810
But anyway, it was formative for me.

47
00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.730
You know, I often say it's the first time

48
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.560
I actually saw on film a family going through

49
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.810
that very tough thing of the father has left,

50
00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.960
the kids are having to deal with where's daddy gone,

51
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.160
the mother is having to deal with that as well,

52
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.520
and then, whoosh, the adventure and the fantasy begins.

53
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.160
And Spielberg more than anyone, you know,

54
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.780
has just done that so brilliantly.

55
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.860
I managed to get my film to him

56
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.600
and he watched "The Water Man" and wrote back to me

57
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.210
and said, "You're a fine director

58
00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.770
and a really good storyteller."

59
00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:46.410
And I was just like, oh, my goodness.

60
00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.305
Life is so weird.

61
00:02:48.305 --> 00:02:51.338
But, anyway.
(audience applauds)

62
00:02:51.338 --> 00:02:53.150
That makes me so happy.

63
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.600
He'll be honest, too, I do believe.

64
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.440
Like, he is that kind of person.

65
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.650
Yeah, you know, I have four kids now,

66
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.650
and to get to share these kinds of stories with them

67
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:04.740
is so precious to me,

68
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.590
because I had to go back to those films

69
00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.910
in order to share a narrative like this to them.

70
00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.130 line:15%
So, when "The Water Man" came along,

71
00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.780 line:15%
I thought, gosh, this could be something like that

72
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.150 line:15%
for a modern audience.

73
00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.960 line:15%
We actually have a clip from "The Water Man",

74
00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.790
and I was wondering if you could kick it old school for us

75
00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.310
and help us set it up.

76
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.260
Okay.

77
00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.560
Is it a clip between me and Lonnie?

78
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:27.480
Guess what?

79
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.300
I'm about to surprise you with it.

80
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.230
Hold on, watch this.

81
00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.293
Okay, okay.

82
00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:37.080
If you had to choose between a short life

83
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.448
with a lot of love in it

84
00:03:40.448 --> 00:03:45.290
and a very long life with no one left to love,

85
00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.760
which one would you choose?

86
00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.430
I would take a short life with your mom in it

87
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.093
than a long one without her.

88
00:04:06.578 --> 00:04:07.411
Me, too.

89
00:04:09.047 --> 00:04:10.180
(audience applauds)

90
00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.260
I'm just so excited for this movie.

91
00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.700
Okay, can I ask you another question?

92
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.870
I was reading in your life story

93
00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.250 line:15%
that you had had a point where you thought

94
00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:25.640 line:15%
maybe you would follow in your father's footsteps in life,

95
00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.593
and he had gotten married when he was 40 years old,

96
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.860
but you met the love of your life when you were,

97
00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.463
what was the exact age?

98
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.640
We were 17 and 18 when we first met.

99
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.780
Okay, and I was wondering,

100
00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.980
what would you say to someone who

101
00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.580
their life surprised them

102
00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.733
much earlier than they had planned?

103
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.960
Oh, it's a beautiful question, actually,

104
00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.470
because, as you say, my dad was my hero,

105
00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:57.600
and so, basically, I was going to follow in his footsteps,

106
00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.270
and then life happened.

107
00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:02.240 line:15%
I met this woman and, you know,

108
00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.040 line:15%
we became friends initially,

109
00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.780 line:15%
and then I just got to the point

110
00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.300
where I couldn't imagine the rest of my life

111
00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:12.240
without her in it, and I thought, oh, no.

112
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.180
It's gone and happened way earlier than I thought it would.

113
00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.843
Is it true that the morning after your wedding,

114
00:05:19.843 --> 00:05:24.240
there was a towel. (laughs)

115
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.590
Okay, so I am,

116
00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:28.933
I am anally tidy.

117
00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.500
My wife's name is Jessica,

118
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.380
and she grew up being called messy Jessie.

119
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.130
So, you know, we had a beautiful wedding.

120
00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.170
We had our wedding night, and it's the next morning,

121
00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:44.950
and my new wife, my brand new wife goes in the shower.

122
00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:46.470
She's had a shower.

123
00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.190
She comes in and she takes off her towel

124
00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:52.870
and she throws it on the floor and presents herself

125
00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.090
in all of her nakedness,

126
00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:59.125
and the first thing I did was go (yells)

127
00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.475
I was looking at the towel,

128
00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:06.330
and she thought, oh, dear, we may have a problem here.

129
00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.260
I love your love story with your wife so much

130
00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.068
and I just wanted to celebrate it.

131
00:06:11.068 --> 00:06:13.651 line:15%
(upbeat music)

